    Q("FOR-07", "forests", "3.5.3.3", "360-369, 386-387", title="Sustainable forestry at Rio Bravo, Belize",
      intro="The Rio Bravo Conservation and Management Area in Belize is a largely undamaged rainforest with over 200 species of "
            "tree, 400 species of bird and 70 species of mammal. It is managed by a Belizean NGO to conserve wildlife, produce "
            "resources, sequester carbon and benefit the local economy.",
      parts=[
          P("Explain why creating the reserve could have threatened local communities, and describe how the project avoids this.", 3,
            ms=["Local people had used the area for fuelwood, timber for house building, clearings for farming, hunting and the collection of medicines and fibres, all of which protection could have stopped",
                "Local people are employed as rangers, forestry workers and in ecotourism, so the forest is worth more to them standing than cleared",
                "Ecotourist groups eat in local restaurants, are fed on local produce and buy locally made souvenirs, spreading the income through the community"]),
          P("Explain <b>four</b> of the rules followed when trees are selected and felled that keep the exploitation sustainable.", 4,
            ms=["Trees are only felled where other trees of the same species stand upwind of the clearing, so their seed will blow in and recolonise it",
                "Small trees are left standing so that they can grow and be harvested in the future when they are larger",
                "Selected large trees are left because they produce many seeds that fill the gaps left by the trees that were felled",
                "No mechanical or harvesting activity takes place near rivers, because aquatic ecosystems are ecologically sensitive",
                "The use of large machines is restricted because they damage the trunks and roots of the trees that are not being felled",
                "Track routes are planned to avoid important trees and their roots, including rare trees and those that feed monkeys or birds, and old tracks are re-used"]),
          P("Each felled tree is given a unique identification tag. Explain how this supports Forest Stewardship Council accreditation.", 2,
            ms=["The tag allows the timber to be tracked from the stump where it grew to its final retail destination, such as a DIY store in the UK",
                "That chain of custody is the evidence the FSC needs that the wood really came from a sustainably managed forest and was not mixed with illegally logged timber"]),
          P("Describe <b>three</b> ways the reserve raises money without damaging the forest.", 3,
            ms=["Ecotourism: visitors pay to see the forest and its wildlife, and local businesses supply them",
                "Timber confiscated from illegal loggers by the rangers is sold, and the money pays for conservation work",
                "Electricity companies, airlines and individuals fund an area of forest as a carbon sequestration research project, because they want to offset atmospheric carbon dioxide",
                "Lower-value species such as zericote are developed for furniture, musical instruments and carving, and small branches and offcuts are given to local artisans"]),
          P("Explain why selective logging of this kind causes much less ecological damage than clear felling.", 3,
            ms=["The canopy is only opened in small gaps, so the forest microclimate - shade, humidity and temperature - is maintained and shade-dependent species survive",
                "The soil keeps its cover of vegetation and leaf litter, and the roots that bind it, so erosion, leaching and sedimentation of rivers are far lower",
                "Seed trees and small trees remain, so the gaps are recolonised by the same species and the forest regenerates naturally without replanting",
                "Habitats and food sources are left in place and the forest is not fragmented, so populations of birds and mammals are not isolated"]),
      ]),

    Q("FS-08", "food-species", "3.5.1.2", "321-328", title="Energy ratios and the manipulation of food species",
      intro="Table 1 shows the energy inputs and food energy outputs of three farming systems, in GJ ha^-1 yr^-1.",
      figures=[Table(["System", "Energy input / GJ ha^-1 yr^-1", "Food energy output / GJ ha^-1 yr^-1"],
                     [["Subsistence maize, hand cultivated", "0.6", "9.0"],
                      ["Intensive wheat, UK", "18.0", "54.0"],
                      ["Intensive beef, feedlot", "40.0", "12.0"]])],
      parts=[
          P("Calculate the energy ratio (output / input) for each system in Table 1.", 3, calc=True,
            ms=["Subsistence maize: 9.0 / 0.6 = 15", "Intensive wheat: 54.0 / 18.0 = 3", "Feedlot beef: 12.0 / 40.0 = 0.3"]),
          P("Explain what is meant by an <b>energy subsidy</b>, and use your answers to explain why the intensive systems have much lower energy ratios.", 3,
            ms=["An energy subsidy is the energy put into a farming system by people rather than by the Sun: fuel for machinery, the manufacture of fertilisers and pesticides, irrigation, transport, drying and storage",
                "Intensive systems raise the yield per hectare by adding large energy subsidies, so the output rises but the input rises faster and the ratio falls",
                "The feedlot ratio is below 1, so it takes more energy to produce the beef than the beef contains; the extra trophic level means most of the energy in the feed is lost in respiration, excretion and the inedible parts of the animal"]),
          P("Explain <b>three</b> ways a farmer can reduce the energy losses in a food production chain.", 3,
            ms=["Keep the food chain short by producing crops for direct human consumption rather than feeding them to livestock, so one transfer of about 10% efficiency is avoided",
                "Restrict the movement of livestock, so less of the energy in the feed is used for muscular activity and more is converted to biomass",
                "Keep the animals warm, or rear them indoors, so less energy is spent maintaining body temperature",
                "Slaughter the animals young, while their growth rate and food conversion ratio are still high",
                "Use species or breeds with a high food conversion ratio, such as poultry or farmed fish, rather than cattle",
                "Control parasites and disease, and use feed that is easy to digest, so more of the food eaten is assimilated"]),
          P("Explain <b>two</b> advantages of genetic modification over conventional selective breeding.", 2,
            ms=["A single desired gene can be transferred, so the characteristic appears without the unwanted genes that come with a whole parent in conventional breeding",
                "Genes can be moved between species that could never interbreed, so characteristics can be introduced that selective breeding could never produce, and the result is achieved in one generation instead of many"]),
          P("Explain <b>four</b> concerns about the use of GM crops.", 4,
            ms=["Genes may escape by pollen transfer to wild relatives or to neighbouring non-GM crops, creating herbicide-resistant weeds or making organic certification impossible",
                "Insect-resistant crops may kill non-target species, including the pollinators and natural pest predators that the wider ecosystem depends on",
                "Pests and weeds evolve resistance to the toxin or the herbicide the crop is designed to be used with, so the advantage is temporary and use of the chemical increases",
                "Reliance on a few patented GM varieties narrows the genetic diversity of the crop, making it more vulnerable to a new disease",
                "Seed is patented and must be bought each year, which can put subsistence farmers in debt or make them dependent on one supplier",
                "Long-term effects on human health are not fully known, and labelling may not let consumers choose"]),
      ]),

